{"kind":"report","text":"C1 ruling: a hand-recorded post has its own state, and retirement is final with one result per write\n\n1. `record-posting`. Add an eighth post state, `posted`. The act applies only to an approved post whose channel dap cannot write to. A post on the site's Git channel takes `publish` and never `record-posting`. The act stores the link, the signing member and the commit time, and moves the post from approved to posted. It never writes `requestOutcome`, which stays pending, so the record makes no claim of admission. The same act moves the post's slot to done. The slot is the deadline, and the owner's signed record meets it. A post that stays overdue after the owner has posted it would mislead the owner every day. The card and the carousel show \"posted by hand by \u003cmember\u003e, \u003ctime\u003e\" with the link. The admitted-publications log (e7152200) still lists only admitted posts.\n\n2. `retire`. Accept your proposal: the post moves to retired at once, and retired stays final. Keep any earlier `requestOutcome` and admission time for the log. Send the notice and the index rewrite only when the post's outcome is admitted. A planned, drafted, approved or posted post has no file on the site, so its retirement sends nothing. Each send writes its own result entry. The card and the log line show both results by name, and a refused write shows its reason. If either write is refused, nothing may read as a successful retirement publication. C1 has no retry act. Name that limit in the implementation note: after a refused retirement write, the owner repairs the site by hand. Do not add a separate retirement item.\n\n3. The slot under `retire`. Add one slot state, `dropped`. Retiring a post whose slot is not done moves that slot to dropped. Dropped escapes the overdue rule and leaves the carousel. A slot that is already done stays done. The one-post-per-slot range keeps counting retired posts, and the 24-post bound still includes them. To replace a withdrawn post, the owner schedules a new slot.\n\nThese rulings change only C1's own definitions. No shared source changes."}